WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.906
Colour can make a deck look considered, or make it look messy.

2
00:00:04.956 --> 00:00:10.237
Here's how to build a small palette that reads well on laptops, phones and in video.

3
00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:20.324
Most slide palettes trip over the same things: too many colours, brand colours everywhere, weak contrast, and colour carrying meaning on its own.

4
00:00:20.824 --> 00:00:23.410
Think in roles, not favourite colours.

5
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:30.248
A background, text, a couple of greys, one accent for what matters and a quiet support colour.

6
00:00:30.298 --> 00:00:32.374
That's five to seven in total.

7
00:00:32.874 --> 00:00:36.655
Colours that look fine on your monitor can wash out on a projector.

8
00:00:36.705 --> 00:00:42.531
Aim for 4.5 to 1 contrast for body text, and label your chart series directly.

9
00:00:43.274 --> 00:00:46.673
Take a chart with six bars in six brand colours.

10
00:00:46.723 --> 00:00:51.783
Grey out five, put the north in the accent, and the slide says exactly one thing.

11
00:00:53.033 --> 00:00:56.071
Solid, distinct colours survive compression.

12
00:00:56.121 --> 00:01:01.145
On Pro, a.i. directs your video, landing your accent on the word that names it.

13
00:01:01.195 --> 00:01:04.594
Try Basic first at powerpoint2vid.com.

